492344856262, 0.005225038155913353, 0.014181281439960003, -0.030850032344460487, 0.0010924926027655602, -0.0013434364227578044, 0.01558724232017994, -0.007908531464636326, 0.014248874969780445, -0.007077121641486883, 0.020143097266554832, -0.02774069458246231, 0.0057455142959952354, 0.0004909035051241517, -0.021035341545939445, 0.0071244374848902225, -0.01631726138293743, 0.0036669899709522724, 0.025969725102186203]], "input": ["# European Commission and Food Regulation Community\n\nThe community is centered around the European Commission, the European Parliament, and the European Union, all of which play significant roles in regulating food safety and labeling standards within the EU. Their interrelationships highlight a collaborative effort to ensure consumer protection, particularly concerning foods intended for athletes.\n\n## The European Commission's regulatory authority\n\nThe Comisión Europea, or European Commission, is the executive body of the EU responsible for proposing legislation and implementing decisions. It plays a crucial role in food safety, particularly through its oversight of food labeling regulations and nutritional information. Recently, it presented a report to the European Parliament regarding foods intended for athletes, emphasizing its commitment to maintaining high standards in food safety and transparency. This regulatory authority is vital for ensuring that food products meet safety standards and that consumers are well-informed about the products they purchase [Data: Entities (953); Relationships (5421, 5424, 5422, 5423, 5426, +more)].\n\n## The European Parliament's legislative role\n\nThe Parlamento Europeo, or European Parliament, is a key legislative institution that defines and regulates food hygiene standards within the EU. It has adopted regulations concerning food labeling, which are essential for consumer protection. The Parliament's engagement with the Comisión Europea on reports about athlete-targeted foods highlights its active role in shaping food regulation policies. This collaboration is crucial for ensuring that food safety measures are effectively implemented and that consumers receive accurate information about food products [Data: Entities (863); Relationships (5152, 5154, 5155, +more)].\n\n## The European Union's overarching framework\n\nThe European Union (EU) serves as the political and economic union that encompasses the regulatory frameworks established by the European Commission and the European Parliament. It plays a significant role in harmonizing food standards across member states, including dietary recommendations for sodium and potassium intake. The EU's efforts to implement and harmonize legislation related to food labeling are critical for public health and consumer safety, particularly in the context of foods aimed at athletes [Data: Entities (7794); Relationships (16737, 16742, +more)].\n\n## Importance of food labeling regulations\n\nReglamento (UE) Nº 1169/2011 is a legal framework that governs the information related to food that must be provided to consumers. This regulation is essential for ensuring that consumers receive clear and accurate information about food products, thereby promoting informed choices and enhancing food safety. The European Parliament's adoption of this regulation underscores the importance of transparency in food labeling, which is particularly relevant for consumers, including athletes who require specific dietary information [Data: Entities (7908); Relationships (5153, 16830, 16827, +more)].\n\n## Collaboration with health organizations\n\nThe Comisión Europea collaborates with various health organizations, including the World Health Organization, to promote health initiatives across Europe. This collaboration is significant for addressing public health concerns related to food safety and nutrition. The European Commission's alignment with health guidelines, such as those provided by NAS on sodium intake, reflects its commitment to integrating health considerations into food regulation policies [Data: Relationships (5172, 16751, +more)].", "# EU Food Regulation Framework\n\nThe community is centered around key European Union regulations that govern nutritional claims and food additives. The entities are interconnected through various relationships that highlight their compliance and regulatory framework, which is crucial for food safety and consumer information.\n\n## Reglamento (UE) Nº 1924/2006 as a foundational regulation\n\nReglamento (UE) Nº 1924/2006 establishes the rules for nutritional and health claims on food products, making it a cornerstone of food regulation in the EU. This regulation is critical as it ensures that consumers receive accurate information about the health benefits of food products, which can influence consumer choices and public health outcomes. The regulation's relationships with other EU directives, such as Real Decreto 1205/2010 and Reglamento (UE) Nº 1169/2011, further emphasize its importance in the regulatory landscape. [Data: Entities (16063); Relationships (9969, 16830, 20423, 20424)]\n\n## Interconnection with food additive regulations\n\nReglamento (UE) Nº 1333/2008, which lists permitted food additives in the EU, is closely related to Reglamento (UE) Nº 1924/2006. This relationship highlights the importance of ensuring that nutritional claims are supported by safe and approved additives, thereby protecting consumer health. The interplay between these regulations ensures that food products not only make valid claims but also adhere to safety standards, which is essential for maintaining public trust in food products. [Data: Entities (16064); Relationships (20423)]\n\n## Health claims regulation under Reglamento (UE) Nº 2016/1413\n\nReglamento (UE) Nº 2016/1413 establishes authorized health claims for food products, which are relevant to the nutritional claims regulated by Reglamento (UE) Nº 1924/2006. This regulation ensures that any health claims made by food products are scientifically substantiated, thereby preventing misleading information that could harm consumers. The relationship between these two regulations is vital for ensuring that consumers can trust the health benefits advertised on food packaging. [Data: Entities (16067); Relationships (20424)]\n\n## Real Decreto 1205/2010's reliance on EU regulations\n\nReal Decreto 1205/2010 is based on EU regulations, including Reglamento (UE) Nº 1924/2006, for the inclusion of dietary foods in the National Health System. This connection illustrates how EU regulations influence national legislation, ensuring that dietary foods meet specific health standards before being integrated into public health systems. The reliance on EU regulations underscores the importance of compliance at both the EU and national levels for food safety and public health. [Data: Relationships (9969)]\n\n## Complementary role of Reglamento (UE) Nº 1169/2011\n\nReglamento (UE) Nº 1169/2011 complements Reglamento (UE) Nº 1924/2006 by regulating the information that must be provided to consumers about food products. This regulation is crucial for transparency and consumer rights, ensuring that consumers are well-informed about the products they purchase. The relationship between these two regulations highlights the comprehensive approach taken by the EU to safeguard consumer interests in the food sector. [Data: Relationships (16830)]", "# Dietary Reference Intakes and Nutritional Guidelines\n\nThe community focuses on Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs) and their application across various population groups, including children, adults, and pregnant individuals. Key entities include authors of food composition tables and organizations responsible for establishing these guidelines, highlighting the interconnectedness of nutritional science and public health.\n\n## Dietary Reference Intakes as a foundational framework\n\nDietary Reference Intakes (DRIs) serve as a comprehensive framework for assessing and planning nutrient intake across various demographics. These guidelines are essential for ensuring that individuals meet their nutritional needs, thereby promoting overall health and preventing deficiencies. The DRI encompasses various age groups and specific populations, such as children and pregnant individuals, indicating its broad applicability and importance in public health. [Data: Entities (7777); Relationships (16713, 14118, 16704, 16705, 11760, +more)]\n\n## Diverse population groups targeted by dietary guidelines\n\nThe DRI includes specific recommendations for various population groups, such as children aged 1-3 years, adults aged 31-50 years, and pregnant individuals. This targeted approach ensures that nutritional needs are met according to the unique requirements of each demographic, which is crucial for maintaining health and preventing diet-related diseases. The inclusion of diverse groups highlights the importance of tailored dietary recommendations in public health initiatives. [Data: Entities (16521, 16522, 16526, 16515, 16516, +more); Relationships (16721, 16722, 16715, 16716, 16717, +more)]\n\n## Contributions of key authors to nutritional science\n\nAuthors such as Angelles Carbajal, Carmen Cuadrado, Luisa Cabrera, and Olga Moreiras have significantly contributed to the establishment of dietary reference intakes through their work on food composition tables. Their research and findings are integral to the development of guidelines that inform public health policies and dietary recommendations. The collaboration among these experts underscores the importance of scientific research in shaping nutritional guidelines. [Data: Entities (11612, 11614, 11613, 11615); Relationships (16707, 16709, 16708, 16710, +more)]\n\n## Role of Lancet Oncology in dietary research\n\nLancet Oncology plays a significant role in publishing research related to dietary reference intakes and their impact on cancer risk. This connection between diet and cancer highli